  "query_preview": "..\nThe track for Tropical Storm Rachel continues to have minor \nshifts as it continues to interact with the upper-level trough \nthat is deepening in the Pacific Northwest, but the overall track \ncontinues to take the tropical moisture mainly over eastern \nCalifornia and into Nevada, western Arizona and Utah. Shower and \nthunderstorm coverage will increase Saturday afternoon as tropical\nmoisture ahead of Rachel moves into region and daytime heating \nsupports the development of convection along the higher terrain. \n\nThe main forecast challenge is determining if most of the\nrainfall over the next few days will be associated with \nscattered showers and thunderstorms with high rain rates or more \nof a steady rainfall event with more widespread rain but more \nmoderate rain rates over a longer period of time. While both \nwould support flooding, the former would support more localized \nrapid flash flooding, while the latter would trend toward a more \nslowly development of flooding that is more related to high water \nin streams and rivers affecting low water crossings and ponding \nof water in poor drainage areas and on roads. The latest track of\nTropical Storm Rachel has westward compared to yesterday, and hi-\nres models show some drier mid-level air wrapping around the \nremnants of Rachel and into Arizona. This would prevent a steady, \nwidespread rainfall and greater cloud cover developing Sunday, but\ninstead continuing to support more of an scattered shower and \nthunderstorm pattern, potentially leading to more variable \nrainfall totals and localized flash flooding. This drier air would\nalso support the mixing down of stronger winds aloft, supporting \nmore widespread area of southerly winds around 20 to 30 mph, \ngusting up to 50 mph across northern Arizona, and increase the \nuncertainty of how heavy rainfall will be Monday and Tuesday as \nthe main trough moves over Arizona.\n\nCurrent forecast is taking a blend of the two scenarios, with QPF\nof 1-3\" along the upsloping (south) side of Mogollon Rim, which \ncould see locally higher amounts and up toward the Grand Canyon \narea and to the Utah border, based on the the path of the greater \ntropical moisture. A Flood Watch is in effect Saturday afternoon \nthrough late Sunday night for Yavapai, western Coconino, northern \nGila, and far southern Navajo counties. The strongest winds and \ngusts are expected Sunday so a Wind Advisory is in effect 8 PM MST\nSunday to 8 PM MST Sunday and will be evaluated to see if \nportions of the advisory need to be extended for some of the \nhigher terrain Monday, depending on how the synoptic pattern \nevolves.",
